Feature: IR interchange # features/ir_interchange.feature:1
  As a Morphir tooling user
  I want to inspect and convert versioned morphir-ir.json files
  So that I can move models between IR format 3.0.0 and draft IR v4
  Scenario: Inspect a morphir-elm format 3 file            # features/ir_interchange.feature:6
    Given the golden fixture "elm-simpleValueTree.json"    # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:31
    When I run "ir inspect" on it                          # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:46
    Then the command exits with code 0                     # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:81
    And the output contains "format: 3.0.0"                # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:87
    And the output contains "kind: Library"                # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:87
    And the output contains "package: morphir/example/app" # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:87

  Scenario: Upgrade a format 3 file to draft v4 and back    # features/ir_interchange.feature:14
    Given the golden fixture "elm-simpleValueTree.json"     # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:31
    When I convert it to "v4"                               # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:52
    Then the command exits with code 0                      # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:81
    And the converted file declares formatVersion 4         # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:99
    When I inspect the converted file                       # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:68
    Then the output contains "draft"                        # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:87
    When I convert the converted file to "v3"               # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:74
    Then the command exits with code 0                      # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:81
    And the converted file has the same JSON as the fixture # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:105

  Scenario: Unversioned IR is rejected                # features/ir_interchange.feature:25
    Given a file with the JSON '{"distribution": []}' # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:38
    When I run "ir inspect" on it                     # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:46
    Then the command exits with code 1                # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:81
    And the errors contain "missing_format_version"   # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:93

  Scenario: Specs cannot be downgraded                                                                 # features/ir_interchange.feature:31
    Given a file with the JSON '{"formatVersion": 4, "distribution": {"Specs": {"packageName": "a"}}}' # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:38
    When I convert it to "v3"                                                                          # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:52
    Then the command exits with code 1                                                                 # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:81
    And the errors contain "unsupported_v4_downgrade"                                                  # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:93
    And no converted file exists                                                                       # features/steps/ir_interchange_steps.py:113

Feature: Morphir SDK # features/morphir_sdk.feature:1
  As a developer of Morphir models
  I want a Python runtime for the Morphir SDK
  So that compiled models behave as they do in Elm
  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.1   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:12
    When I import the SDK module "basics"                           # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.2   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:13
    When I import the SDK module "char"                             # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.3   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:14
    When I import the SDK module "string"                           # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.4   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:15
    When I import the SDK module "list"                             # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.5   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:16
    When I import the SDK module "dict"                             # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.6   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:17
    When I import the SDK module "set"                              # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.7   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:18
    When I import the SDK module "maybe"                            # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.8   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:19
    When I import the SDK module "result"                           # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.9   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:20
    When I import the SDK module "tuple"                            # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                         # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.10   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:21
    When I import the SDK module "decimal"                           # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                          # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.11   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:22
    When I import the SDK module "int"                               # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                          # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario Outline: Every core SDK module can be imported -- @1.12   # features/morphir_sdk.feature:23
    When I import the SDK module "number"                            # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:23
    Then the SDK module should be available                          # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:29

  Scenario: A dictionary lists its entries in key order          # features/morphir_sdk.feature:25
    Given an SDK dictionary built from the pairs "b=2, a=1, c=3" # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:36
    When I list the keys of the dictionary                       # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:48
    Then the keys should be "a, b, c"                            # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:54

  Scenario: Two dictionaries built in a different order are equal  # features/morphir_sdk.feature:30
    Given an SDK dictionary built from the pairs "b=2, a=1"        # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:36
    And another SDK dictionary built from the pairs "a=1, b=2"     # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:42
    Then the two dictionaries should be equal                      # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:60

  Scenario: Rounding follows Elm and not Python  # features/morphir_sdk.feature:35
    When I round the float 2.5 with the SDK      # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:67
    Then the rounded value should be 3           # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:73

  Scenario: Decimal arithmetic is exact                  # features/morphir_sdk.feature:39
    When I add the decimals "0.1" and "0.2" with the SDK # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:79
    Then the decimal result should print as "0.3"        # features/steps/sdk_steps.py:87

Feature: Morphir Python Setup # features/morphir_setup.feature:1
  As a developer
  I want to verify the project is set up correctly
  So that I can start developing Morphir features
  Scenario: Morphir core library can be imported  # features/morphir_setup.feature:6
    Given the Python environment is set up        # features/steps/setup_steps.py:13
    When I import the morphir module              # features/steps/setup_steps.py:22
    Then the module should be available           # features/steps/setup_steps.py:46
    And it should have a version attribute        # features/steps/setup_steps.py:52

  Scenario: Morphir tools can be imported  # features/morphir_setup.feature:12
    Given the Python environment is set up # features/steps/setup_steps.py:13
    When I import the morphir_tools module # features/steps/setup_steps.py:30
    Then the module should be available    # features/steps/setup_steps.py:46
    And it should have a version attribute # features/steps/setup_steps.py:52

  Scenario: Morphir IR module can be imported  # features/morphir_setup.feature:18
    Given the Python environment is set up     # features/steps/setup_steps.py:13
    When I import the morphir.ir module        # features/steps/setup_steps.py:38
    Then the module should be available        # features/steps/setup_steps.py:46

